# The difference of two intervals with one-limb bounds (milestone2.md, "Interval kernels", "One
# limb"): [xl - yu, xu - yl], outward, by the directed differences; the empty set when an operand
# is empty. The same statement as the sum, of x - y.
kernel:        interval_sub_1
input:         stored xl
input:         stored xu
input:         stored yl
input:         stored yu
output:        zl
output:        zu
requires:      interval xl xu
requires:      interval yl yu
ensures:       encloses_interval zl zu x-y x=xl,xu y=yl,yu tight=rounded bounded_when=abs_le(x-y,omega) bounded_exactly
min_precision: 2
source:        directed differences of the bounds (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeInterval1.v interval_sub_1_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the bounds are directed differences, which select, and the empty set is selected after an explicit test; encloses_interval states what comes out

l  = sub_down_1(xl, yu)
h  = sub_up_1(xu, yl)
nx = lt(xl, const inf)
ny = lt(yl, const inf)
ne = and(nx, ny)
mi = neg(const inf)
zl = select(ne, l, const inf)
zu = select(ne, h, mi)
